    </dashboard>
    <dashboard name='Story 1' type='storyboard'>
      <layout-options>
        <title>
          <formatted-text>
            <run>Linear trends, power and mileage</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </title>
      </layout-options>
      <style>
        <style-rule element='story-point-caption'>
          <format attr='width' value='691' />
        </style-rule>
      </style>
      <size maxheight='964' maxwidth='1016' minheight='964' minwidth='1016' />
      <zones>
        <zone h='100000' id='2' type-v2='layout-basic' w='100000' x='0' y='0'>
          <zone h='98340' id='1' param='vert' removable='false' type-v2='layout-flow' w='98426' x='787' y='830'>
            <zone h='3423' id='3' type-v2='title' w='98426' x='787' y='830' />
            <zone fixed-size='84' h='8714' id='4' is-fixed='true' paired-zone-id='5' removable='false' type-v2='flipboard-nav' w='98426' x='787' y='4253' />
            <zone h='86203' id='5' paired-zone-id='4' removable='false' type-v2='flipboard' w='98426' x='787' y='12967'>
              <flipboard active-id='5' nav-type='caption' show-nav-arrows='true'>
                <story-points>
                  <story-point caption='The present dataset contains a large number of correlated numerical features. While manufacturer seems to be the best linear predictor for price, engine features such as mechanics and power, as well as car dimensions and mileage also show strong positive or negative trends. When assessing relationships with price, Citympg is preferred to Highwaympg due to its stronger and clearer negative linear trend.' captured-sheet='Price 1' id='1' />
                  <story-point caption='Here you can see the downward trendlines between city and highway miles per galon - while these seem almost parallel, the linear trend is stronger for citympg than highway mpg (making it a more suitable choice for linear reggression). In fact, both trends may be interptreted as hyperbolic, or may be fitted with polynomials of degrees 3 and 4, respectively - but this kind of variability may warn of overfitting.' captured-sheet='Price MPG' id='2' />
                  <story-point caption='Some car features occur concommitantly as others, without necessarily showing a numerical relationshop between them. For example, both the carbody and drivewheel describe the car body&apos;s configuration. We wanted to see how these aspects, coupled with the enginetype (engine design), are indicative of the average price.' captured-sheet='Price 2' id='3' />
                  <story-point caption='In a separate investigation, we are exploring relationships between car body, engine type, and how these group together to affect trends in city mileage and horsepower (for example, hatchbacks show both best city mileage and horsepower, although the trend between mileage and horsepower is negative, as shown in the next sheet).' captured-sheet='Misc' id='4' />
                  <story-point caption='This sheet explores relationships between horsepower and various features such as mileage, enginesize, peak revolutions per minute, number of engine combustion chambers, and the compression ratio (combustion chamber ratio, from largest bottom to smallest top piston). Interestingly, we see no relationship between the number of rpms and horsepower, even if both features define the engine&apos;s performance. There is an almost linear (or hyperbolic) downward trend between mileage and horsepower, and a positive linear relationship between enginesize and horsepower, as expected. Features such as compressionratio and cylinder number don&apos;t seem to affect horsepower, but we can see the limitations imposed on these features by engine manufacturing, in the straight bands appearing in the charts.' captured-sheet='Power' id='5' />
                </story-points>
              </flipboard>
            </zone>
          </zone>
          <zone-style>
            <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
            <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
            <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
            <format attr='margin' value='8' />
          </zone-style>
        </zone>
        <zone flipboard-zone-id='5' h='5602' hidden='true' id='6' story-point-id='2' type-v2='text' w='33268' x='48524' y='11100'>
          <formatted-text>
            <run fontalignment='0' fontsize='10'>By ticking manufacturer boxes, the viewer can check for themselves the price concentration of each manufacturer, in each scatterplot.</run>
          </formatted-text>
        </zone>
      </zones>
      <simple-id uuid='{016CF8B3-1A77-467E-B635-F94F175BF00E}' />
    </dashboard>
  </dashboards>
